    a text called 'later handle' is 'gen buffer reference' of 'later stem'.
    a text called 'the second line' is "Get current time into {'later handle'}".
    a text called 'the earlier unix shown' is 'gen buffer slot of' of "{stem}'s unix".
    a text called 'the monotonic check' is 'gen assert time at least' of "TIM-11" and "{'later stem'}'s unix" and "{stem}'s unix" and 'the earlier unix shown'.
    a text called 'the inline hour check' is 'gen assert time bounded' of "TIM-13" and "current time's hour" and "0" and "23".
    a list called 'the tail lines' is ['the second line', 'the monotonic check', 'the inline hour check'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the tail lines'.

(One phrasing of `Start`/`Stop`, drawn from all four contextual forms the
 manual documents as equal (TIM-24, TIM-25, TIM-26, TIM-27,
 LANGUAGE.md:4625, 4631-4636): canonical or alternate spelling, with or
 without the article, in any capitalisation the two spellings actually
 use. All eight combinations hand-verified to parse and behave the same
 (`Start the crank.`, `stop crank.`, `Begin the 'job clock'.`, `finish
 'job clock'.` among them) - the manual's own claim is that these are
 ordinary contextual words, not that any one spelling is special, and
 today's corpus emits exactly one shape of each, which is the "always
 capitalised, always article-less" invariant the ledger already flags as
