(TIM-15, TIM-16, TIM-17, TIM-18, TIM-19, TIM-49: every accepted
 Wait/Sleep spelling, `for` included or not (LANGUAGE.md:4599-4609). The
 "seconds" flavour always waits 0 (hand-verified: `Wait 5 second.` really
 does wait five real seconds - singular/plural is cosmetic, the compiler
 does not check agreement - so a non-zero seconds draw is not an option
 in a campaign that runs thousands of these and the surface brief caps
 any wait at 200ms); the "milliseconds" flavour carries the real
 magnitude the leaf measures against. Returns the requested duration
 in milliseconds alongside the line so the caller can build the bound
 without re-deriving it.)

(`'seconds flavour'` is the CALLER's decision, not redrawn here - it also
 decided whether `'the requested milliseconds'` is 0 or a real magnitude, and the
 assertion built from that same value has to match what this function
 actually renders. A second, independent coin flip in here for the same
 choice was the bug that shipped first: it could pick "seconds" (amount
 forced to 0) while the caller's own draw had already set `'the
 requested milliseconds'` to a real magnitude, rendering `Wait 0
 seconds.` next to an assertion that expected the magnitude the
 STATEMENT never used - a
 campaign of 200 caught it inside twenty minutes (TIM-15, seed 20260833:
 "expected at least 21 got 0"). One flag, drawn once, is the fix.)

To 'gen timer wait line' with a number called 'the requested milliseconds' and a boolean called 'seconds flavour'.
    a number called 'the verb draw' is 'rng below' of 2.
    a number called 'the for draw' is 'rng below' of 2.
    a number called 'the plural draw' is 'rng below' of 2.
    a text called verb is "Wait".
    If 'the verb draw' is 1 then,
        Set verb to "Sleep".
    a text called 'the for word' is "".
    If 'the for draw' is 1 then,
        Set 'the for word' to "for ".
    a text called unit is "millisecond".
    If 'the plural draw' is 1 then,
        Set unit to "milliseconds".
    a number called amount is 'the requested milliseconds'.
    If 'seconds flavour' then,
        Set amount to 0.
    If 'seconds flavour' and 'the plural draw' is 0 then,
        Set unit to "second".
    If 'seconds flavour' and 'the plural draw' is 1 then,
        Set unit to "seconds".
    a text called 'the line' is "{verb} {'the for word'}{amount} {unit}".
    Return a text, 'the line'.

(TIM-22, TIM-23, TIM-31, TIM-33, TIM-34, TIM-35, TIM-36, TIM-37, TIM-38:
 a timer's whole lifecycle. Creation is `a timer called <name>.` or
 `Create a timer called <name>.` (TIM-22, TIM-23, LANGUAGE.md:4618-4619),
 name quoting decided by the shared word pool rather than this leaf.
 `running` reads false before Start and after Stop and (checked, not
 asserted here to avoid a third read racing the wait) true between
 (TIM-35, reusing 'gen assert true'/'gen assert false' from
 gen_buffers.vox per PROCEDURE.md 6a - generic despite the name).
 `duration in milliseconds` after the wait is bounded below by what was
 requested and above by a generous cap (5000 ms - a starved box, not a
 hang, is what this leaf tolerates; TIM-15's own bound, and TIM-31's
 "assert the total") - `duration in seconds` is cross-checked against it
 within 1000, which is `floor(ms/1000)`, hand-verified (a 150ms timer
 read `duration in seconds` as 0) (TIM-36, TIM-37, TIM-38). `start time`
 is bracketed below by a `unix` reading taken before Start and `end
 time` above by one taken after Stop, with `end time >= start time`
 between them (TIM-33, TIM-34) - hand-verified exactly this bracket on
 vox 0.4.14 and the 0.4.15 stack.)

