( ── time leaves, batch B, 2026-08-30 night ──

 docs/ledger/time.md, TIM-32, TIM-42, TIM-43, TIM-44, TIM-45, TIM-46,
 TIM-47, TIM-51, TIM-54, TIM-57 - ten of the 21 rows batch A left `todo`,
 picked for being assertable on both vox 0.4.14 (installed) and the
 0.4.15 stack with no reference clock. Kinds 222-231, misc's own
 220-239 band; RAW span 128-137 remaps `kind add 94` (the widening hunk
 that does this lives apart from the registration block below, per the
 common brief, and is discarded at merge).

 Skipped, and why: TIM-12/TIM-14/TIM-20/TIM-40 are composites that sum
 rows already verified above and add little of their own; TIM-28/TIM-29
 need the shared name pool itself to emit a reserved-context word
 (`stop`, `start`, `begin`, `finish`, `end`) as a variable/function name,
 which is a pool change, not one leaf; TIM-31 duplicates TIM-37/TIM-38's
 already-verified cast reads; TIM-39 (`elapsed in seconds`) is the
 cheapest row left over and was cut only to hold this batch at exactly
 ten; TIM-52 (undocumented aliases: `retrieve`/`fetch`, `stopwatch`,
 `pause`, `delay`, `timestamp`/`unixtime`, the plural property spellings)
 needs a spelling pool of its own to vary properly, too big for one
 row's slot here. TIM-44 and TIM-45 below assert exactly the
 relationship Discrepancies 5 and 2 already recorded - the discrepancy
 record says what the compiler DOES, the leaf checks it keeps doing it,
 which is not the same thing as depending on an open discrepancy the way
 TIM-30 (still skipped, still blocked on D1) does.

 Every assertion below prints a fixed verdict on the passing path
 (`"<ID> ok"`) and never a clock-derived value - batch A's own rule
 above, and PROCEDURE.md 6a's lesson about a first version of this
 surface that printed the raw subject and let real scheduler jitter
 through as a `nondeterministic` finding. Every wait below is drawn far
 enough from its neighbour that ordinary jitter cannot flip an
 inequality (each leaf that needs this says so); nothing here waits
 longer than 700 ms.

 New trap this batch found: a plain NUMBER/TEXT compared or printed
 BARE, with no property attached, must be named through 'gen flow
 witness name' (src/gen_flow.vox), not 'gen buffer reference'. Hand-
 verified both ways (this batch's SYN-3 scratch probe): `Print
 "{'pantry'}"` - a bare, single-quoted, single-word name with nothing
 after it - fails to compile on vox 0.4.14 ("Unknown variable:
 'pantry'") and succeeds on the 0.4.15 stack; the shared word pool
 quotes a one-word draw on roughly half its calls (LANGUAGE.md:760 makes
 the quoting optional, not required), and 'gen assert time at
 least'/'at most'/'matches' all wrap their subject in exactly this bare
 slot for the failure message. 'gen flow witness name' writes a
 one-word draw bare instead of quoting it, which is safe on both
 compilers; a name used only WITH a property attached (`{stem}'s hour`,
 `byte 3 of {handle}`) never hits this trap and keeps using 'gen buffer
 name'/'gen buffer reference' exactly as every existing leaf on this
 surface already does.)

(The fourth member of the `gen assert time ...` family: `bounded`/`at
 least`/`at most` compare against a LITERAL or a floor/ceiling shown
 separately; this one compares two RUNTIME expressions for exact
 equality, the shape 'gen assert number matches' and 'gen assert things
 equal' already give the buffer and things surfaces, generalised to this
 surface's own printed-verdict convention instead of printing the raw
 value on the passing path.)

