(TIM-42 (LANGUAGE.md:4695-4697): the Formatted Time Output example -
 three time properties read straight out of format slots with the `:02`
 zero-pad specifier, combined `hh:mm:ss`. Clock-dependent, so this leaf
 checks the SHAPE the zero-pad guarantees rather than any value: a
 `{X:02}:{Y:02}:{Z:02}` string is always exactly 8 bytes with a colon at
 byte 3 and byte 6, whatever the clock reads (hand-verified on both vox
 0.4.14 and the 0.4.15 stack). TIM-41 (`gen leaf format types`,
 gen_text.vox) already checks one slot this way; this is the first leaf
 to combine all three the way the worked example does. A `text` has no
 `size` (PROCEDURE.md 6a), so the rendered clock string is copied into a
 buffer before its length and bytes are read, the same move `gen leaf
 format types` makes.)

To 'gen leaf formatted clock direct' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    a text called stem is 'gen buffer name'.
    a text called handle is 'gen buffer reference' of stem.
    a text called 'the get line' is "Get current time into {handle}".
    a text called 'clock stem' is 'gen buffer name'.
    a text called 'clock handle' is 'gen buffer reference' of 'clock stem'.
    a text called 'the clock line' is "a text called {'clock handle'} is \"{{{stem}'s hour:02}}:{{{stem}'s minute:02}}:{{{stem}'s second:02}}\"".
    a text called 'bytes stem' is 'gen buffer name'.
    a text called 'bytes handle' is 'gen buffer reference' of 'bytes stem'.
    a text called 'the bytes line' is "a buffer called {'bytes handle'} is {'clock handle'}".
    a text called 'the size check' is 'gen assert time bounded' of "TIM-42" and "{'bytes stem'}'s size" and "8" and "8".
    a text called 'the first colon check' is 'gen assert time bounded' of "TIM-42" and "byte 3 of {'bytes handle'}" and "58" and "58".
    a text called 'the second colon check' is 'gen assert time bounded' of "TIM-42" and "byte 6 of {'bytes handle'}" and "58" and "58".
    a list called 'the lines' is ['the get line', 'the clock line', 'the bytes line', 'the size check', 'the first colon check', 'the second colon check'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(TIM-43 (LANGUAGE.md:4703-4709): the named-parts variant of the same
 example - each zero-padded property stored in its own `text` first,
 then the three combined in one more format string. Same 8-byte,
 colon-at-3-and-6 shape as TIM-42, reached through three intermediate
 declarations instead of one. The three holder names are drawn through
 'gen flow witness name': each is interpolated bare, with no property,
 into the combining format string, exactly the trap documented at the
 top of this batch.)

