(TIM-57 (LANGUAGE.md:4556-4564, undocumented): a time property read from
 a plain NUMBER is accepted - a `time` IS a number, its own unix second
 count - so the compiler needs only a numeric operand and decomposes it
 as an epoch second count. Also a memory-safety row: nothing here may
 crash. The literal stays inside 0..999999 (well under one calendar
 year, 31536000) so `hour` doubles as the UTC oracle TIM-44 already
 established and `year` stays the fixed, safely-assertable 1970 the
 manual's own worked example uses.)

